\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every fixed rational $\delta \in (0,1)$, is it NP-hard to distinguish value 1 from value $\leq \delta $ in an explicitly unweighted two-to-one projection game with fixed alphabets [2q] and [q], where every right answer has exactly two preimages?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every fixed rational $\delta \in (0,1)$, is it NP-hard to distinguish value 1 from value $\leq \delta $ in an explicitly unweighted two-to-one projection game with fixed alphabets [2q] and [q], where every right answer has exactly two preimages?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2018/51}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

